Security issues to investigate and fix
A. Profile role escalation
Inspect the profile update policies and all profile-related functions.
A learner must never be able to change their own role to administrator by directly updating their profile.
Requirements:
New registrations must receive the learner role by default.
Learners must not be able to update their role.
Learners must not be able to modify privileged profile fields.
Administrator provisioning must use a secure, controlled process.
Profile update functionality must continue working for legitimate editable fields.
Do not solve this by simply disabling all profile updates.
B. Learning progression manipulation
Inspect all policies and database functions affecting:
Course enrollments.
Course completions.
Lesson progress.
Activity progress.
Quiz attempts.
User skills.
Challenge submissions.
Learners must not be able to directly manipulate database records to award themselves course completion, lesson completion, demonstrated skill levels or assessment approval.
Requirements:
Database-side validation must be authoritative.
Only permitted actions should change progression.
The database must validate the authenticated user, enrollment, lesson/course relationship and completion requirements.
Internal progression functions must not be exposed as unrestricted RPCs.
Prevent users from submitting progress records for another user.
Prevent learners from changing privileged fields such as approval status or reviewer identity.
C. SECURITY DEFINER functions
Inspect every function declared "SECURITY DEFINER".
For each function:
Determine why elevated privileges are needed.
Ensure its "search_path" is explicitly controlled.
Check that it validates the caller's identity and authorization.
Revoke unnecessary execution permissions from "PUBLIC", "anon" and "authenticated".
Grant execution only to the appropriate roles.
Separate internal functions from functions intentionally exposed as RPC endpoints.
Pay particular attention to:
"upgrade_user_skill_level"
"evaluate_course_and_skill_progression"
"submit_quiz_attempt"
"review_submission"
All administrator functions.
All authentication-related triggers and functions.
Do not blindly revoke permissions from every function. Preserve legitimate application functionality through explicitly authorized entry points.
D. Quiz and skill progression integrity
Inspect how quiz results affect skill levels.
A learner passing a knowledge quiz must not automatically receive a practical demonstration achievement unless the assessment genuinely measures practical performance.
Requirements:
Knowledge assessments should update the appropriate knowledge or practice progression.
Practical demonstration should require an approved practical challenge or another explicitly designed practical assessment.
Validate all submitted answers on the server.
Never trust client-submitted scores.
Ensure quiz questions and answer keys are not exposed to unauthorized learners.
Prevent learners from accessing correct answers or explanations before the intended release conditions.
Validate quiz type, question type, answer configuration and scoring rules.
Preserve the existing quiz system and its intended assessment behavior.
E. Submission integrity
Inspect the submission policies, schema and review functions.
Requirements:
Learners can submit their own work.
Learners cannot mark their own submissions as approved.
Learners cannot assign themselves rubric scores.
Learners cannot set reviewer IDs or review timestamps.
Review actions must be administrator-authorized.
Reviewers must not review their own submissions.
Rubric scores must be validated against the challenge rubric.
Submission evidence must belong to the authenticated learner.
F. Course completion rules
Inspect the course progression logic.
Verify that:
Required lessons are correctly identified.
Required activities are correctly identified.
Required quizzes are correctly identified.
Required challenges are correctly identified.
Optional content does not accidentally block completion.
Optional content does not accidentally satisfy required completion conditions.
Completion is calculated consistently and safely.
Duplicate or repeated events do not produce incorrect progression.
G. Storage security
Inspect "supabase/04_storage.sql" and the submission repository.
Requirements:
Evidence storage must remain private.
Learners can upload only to their own permitted storage folder.
Learners cannot read or delete another learner's evidence.
Administrators can access evidence where required for assessment.
File size and allowed file types must be enforced.
Database submission records must reference valid evidence belonging to the submitting learner.
Do not rely on file extensions alone as proof of file content type.
H. Administrator operations
Inspect every administrator RPC and database operation.
Requirements:
Administrator actions must verify administrator privileges on the server.
A client-side admin check is not sufficient.
Validate input IDs and relationships.
Prevent malformed or unauthorized question updates.
Validate phase reordering, including duplicate IDs and missing phases.
Validate course version creation and preserve meaningful version history.
